// Booking System JDL
entity Appointment {
startTime Instant required,
endTime Instant required,
status AppointmentStatus required
}
entity Service {
name String required minlength(3) maxlength(50),
description TextBlob,
price BigDecimal required min(0)
}
enum AppointmentStatus {
REQUESTED, // New status for initial booking request
SCHEDULED, // After admin approval (formerly just SCHEDULED)
COMPLETED,
CANCELLED
}
relationship ManyToOne {
Appointment{user(login) required} to User with builtInEntity,
Appointment{service(name)} to Service
}
service Appointment, Service with serviceClass
paginate Appointment, Service with pagination
dto Appointment, Service with mapstruct
**Explanation (reiterated):**  The extensive updates to the user story describe business rules, UI/UX, and functional/non-functional requirements that are *outside the scope of the JDL*.  The JDL defines the *data model*, not the application logic.  Concepts like preventing overlapping bookings, cancellation policies, email confirmations, admin approval workflows, booking limits, and UI design are all implemented in the application code (Java/TypeScript), not in the JDL.  The provided JDL already contains the necessary data structure.  The implementation effort should focus on building the application logic and UI features described in the updated user story, *using the existing data model defined by this JDL*.  No JDL changes are required.